WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.325
Picture the usual assessment calendar.

2
00:00:02.325 --> 00:00:05.850
The network team reviews the
boundary, keyed by address.

3
00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:10.850
The cloud team runs each provider's
posture scan, keyed by resource tags.

4
00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:14.056
The virtualization team
works a hypervisor checklist,

5
00:00:14.056 --> 00:00:17.262
keyed by whatever the management
plane calls a machine.

6
00:00:17.262 --> 00:00:20.413
At year end, someone puts
the three reports behind

7
00:00:20.413 --> 00:00:23.487
one cover page and calls
it the agency's maturity.

8
00:00:23.487 --> 00:00:26.626
It's really three partial
assessments with one title.

9
00:00:26.626 --> 00:00:28.712
And the failure is architectural.

10
00:00:28.712 --> 00:00:33.807
One database server, running as a virtual
machine, on an agency subnet, backing

11
00:00:33.807 --> 00:00:39.062
up to a cloud bucket, can show up as three
unrelated objects scored by three tools.

12
00:00:39.062 --> 00:00:42.187
The guidance describes the
enterprise, not a tenant.

13
00:00:42.187 --> 00:00:46.193
So unify first, then assess,
and defend that unified plane,

14
00:00:46.193 --> 00:00:50.113
because it becomes a complete
map of the attack surface.

15
00:00:50.113 --> 00:00:52.510
Next, two incidents that show why.
